.Dd November 25, 2026
.Dt BTSPK 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm btspk
.Nd play audio from sndio on a Bluetooth A2DP device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl EJSqv
.Op Fl f Ar firmware.sfi
.Op Fl u Ar unit
.Ar info | reset | scan | upload
.Pp
.Nm
.Op Fl JSqv
.Op Fl F Ar ms
.Op Fl b Ar ms
.Op Fl d Ar device
.Op Fl n Ar frames
.Op Fl u Ar unit
.Ar play Ar bdaddr
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a userland Bluetooth A2DP client for OpenBSD.
It communicates with a Bluetooth controller through
.Xr ugen 4 ,
implements the HCI, ACL, L2CAP and AVDTP protocol paths required for
A2DP playback, encodes PCM audio with SBC, and obtains audio from
.Xr sndio 7 .
.Pp
The default sndio device is
.Pa snd/mon .
The device must provide 16-bit, signed, little-endian, two-channel
PCM at the sample rate selected during A2DP negotiation.
.Pp
.Nm
is intended to operate with Intel USB Bluetooth controllers.
The controller is accessed through
.Pa /dev/ugen
endpoints.
The normal playback process is pledged with
.Xr pledge 2
and its filesystem namespace is restricted with
.Xr unveil 2 .
USB operations which cannot be authorized by
.Xr pledge 2
are handled by a small helper process through a restricted Unix-domain
socket protocol.
.Pp
Unless
.Fl S
is specified, authentication and encryption are required before the
A2DP connection is established.
New pairings require authenticated Secure Simple Pairing with numeric
comparison.
.Fl J
explicitly permits Just Works pairing without MITM protection.
.Sh OPTIONS
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl E
Use the ECDSA Intel firmware image layout when loading firmware.
The default is the RSA layout.
.It Fl F Ar ms
Set the Bluetooth ACL flush timeout in milliseconds.
The valid range is 0 through 1000.
A value of 0 disables the flush timeout.
The default is 100 milliseconds.
.It Fl J
Explicitly allow Just Works pairing.
This pairing method does not provide MITM protection.
The option is mutually exclusive with
.Fl S .
Unauthenticated link keys are accepted and may be stored for later use.
.It Fl S
Skip Bluetooth authentication and encryption.
This is intended for diagnostics only.
The option is mutually exclusive with
.Fl J .
.It Fl b Ar ms
Set the sndio application buffer size in milliseconds.
The valid range is 10 through 60000.
The default is 30 milliseconds.
.It Fl d Ar device
Use the specified sndio device.
Only
.Pa snd/
devices are accepted.
The default is
.Pa snd/mon .
.It Fl f Ar firmware.sfi
Specify an Intel
.Pa .sfi
firmware image.
The image is used when the controller is in its bootloader and
firmware loading is required, or with the
.Ar upload
command.
.It Fl n Ar frames
Set the number of SBC frames placed in each RTP packet.
The valid range is 1 through 15.
The default is 2.
.It Fl q
Suppress informational output and non-fatal warnings.
Fatal errors remain visible.
The Secure Simple Pairing numeric-comparison prompt is not suppressed.
.It Fl u Ar unit
Use the specified
.Xr ugen 4
unit.
The default is 0.
.It Fl v
Enable verbose diagnostic output, including protocol tracing.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
The following commands are available:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar info
Read and display Intel controller firmware information and boot
parameters.
.It Ar reset
Send the Intel controller back to its bootloader.
After the reset, the controller may appear under a different
.Xr ugen 4
unit.
.It Ar scan
Perform a Bluetooth inquiry and display discovered devices, their
class of device and remote name when available.
.It Ar upload
Load the firmware specified by
.Fl f
into a controller which is already in its bootloader.
.It Ar play Ar bdaddr
Establish an ACL connection to the Bluetooth device identified by
.Ar bdaddr ,
negotiate an A2DP SBC stream, and play audio captured from sndio.
The Bluetooth address is written in the usual
.Qq xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
form.
.El
.Sh SECURITY
.Nm
uses several independent OpenBSD security mechanisms.
.Pp
The default Bluetooth pairing policy requires authenticated Secure
Simple Pairing.
The local IO capability is advertised as DisplayYesNo, and numeric
comparison requires explicit confirmation through
.Pa /dev/tty .
Authenticated combination link keys are accepted for subsequent
connections.
.Pp
.Fl J
is an explicit exception to this policy.
It selects NoInputNoOutput capabilities and permits Just Works pairing.
Just Works provides no MITM protection and should only be used when
that limitation is acceptable.
.Pp
.Fl S
is a stronger diagnostic exception: it bypasses authentication and
encryption entirely.
It should not be used on an untrusted Bluetooth link.
.Pp
Link-key files are kept in
.Pa ~/.btspk_keys .
The file must be a regular file owned by the invoking user and must
not be accessible to group or other users.
The file is created with restrictive permissions.
Link-key material is cleared from temporary buffers after use.
.Pp
The main process is pledged after controller setup.
For playback it initially permits:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
stdio rpath wpath cpath unix tty audio
.Ed
.Pp
After
.Xr sio_open 3
has connected to sndiod, the pledge is reduced to:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
stdio unix audio
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Xr audio 4
promise is required by
.Xr sio_open 3
even when the selected device is an sndiod device rather than a
direct
.Pa /dev/audio
device.
.Pp
The scan path uses the narrower:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
stdio unix
.Ed
.Pp
The USB helper is deliberately not pledged because the required
.Xr ugen 4
USB ioctl operations are not covered by a generic pledge promise.
It accepts only the fixed operations required by
.Nm ,
validates HCI command opcodes and lengths, bounds USB transfer data,
and does not accept caller-supplied ioctl numbers.
.Pp
The filesystem namespace is restricted with
.Xr unveil 2 .
The user's home directory is available for the link-key file,
.Pa /dev/tty
is available for numeric comparison, and
.Pa /tmp/sndio
is available for the local sndiod connection.
The unveil namespace is locked before normal protocol processing.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.btspk_keys
Per-user Bluetooth link keys.
.It Pa /dev/ugen Ns Ar unit Ns Pa . Ns Ar endpoint
USB controller endpoints used for HCI and ACL transport.
.It Pa /dev/tty
Used for authenticated Secure Simple Pairing numeric comparison.
.It Pa /tmp/sndio
sndiod Unix-domain socket directory used by sndio devices.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Play audio through the default sndio device:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas btspk play 04:fe:a1:8c:3a:fb
.Ed
.Pp
Allow Just Works pairing explicitly:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas btspk -J play 04:fe:a1:8c:3a:fb
.Ed
.Pp
Scan for Bluetooth devices:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas btspk scan
.Ed
.Pp
Inspect the Intel controller:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas btspk info
.Ed
.Pp
Upload firmware to a controller in its bootloader:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas btspk -f ibt-19-0-4.sfi upload
.Ed
.Pp
Run with reduced output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas btspk -q play 04:fe:a1:8c:3a:fb
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Nm
reports fatal errors on standard error and exits non-zero.
With
.Fl q ,
informational output and non-fatal warnings are suppressed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr ugen 4 ,
.Xr sndio 7
.Sh AUTHORS
The Intel firmware loading path follows the design of
.Nm iwmbtfw ,
including firmware fragment handling.
.Sh CAVEATS
The Intel firmware loader is specific to the controller firmware format
implemented by
.Nm .
The Bluetooth controller must be accessible through
.Xr ugen 4 ,
and the selected sndio device must provide the PCM parameters required
by the negotiated SBC stream.
.Pp
Firmware loading and controller reset can cause the USB device to
reappear under a different
.Xr ugen 4
unit.
